{
  "slug": "d-flip-flop",
  "title": "The D Flip-Flop: From Level to Edge",
  "excerpt": "The storage element every register and counter is actually built from, and why sampling one clock edge instead of a level is what makes chaining them safe.",
  "authorName": "Lance Dy Chua",
  "status": "published",
  "publishedAt": "2026-07-30T09:30:00Z",
  "tags": [
    "sequential",
    "memory"
  ],
  "sectionSlug": "sequential-logic",
  "position": 40,
  "body": [
    {
      "id": "h-intro",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "The D Flip-Flop: From Level to Edge"
    },
    {
      "id": "p-hook",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "From the outside, a D flip-flop looks almost identical to the gated D latch: one data input D, one clock input CLK, outputs Q and Q̄. The behavior inside is completely different, and that difference is exactly the fix the previous tutorial promised."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-edge-vs-level",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Level-Sensitive vs. Edge-Sensitive"
    },
    {
      "id": "p-1",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "A level-sensitive latch cares about a signal's "
        },
        {
          "text": "value",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "italic"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " over some stretch of time (\"while E is high\"). An edge-sensitive flip-flop cares about a signal's "
        },
        {
          "text": "transition",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "italic"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " at one specific moment (\"the instant CLK goes from 0 to 1\", the "
        },
        {
          "text": "rising edge",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "bold"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "), and is completely blind to D the rest of the time. Every instant other than that edge, no matter what D is doing, Q simply holds."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-master-slave",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Built From Two Latches: Master and Slave"
    },
    {
      "id": "p-2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "The circuit below is wired the way real hardware builds one: two gated D latches in series, a "
        },
        {
          "text": "master",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "bold"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " and a "
        },
        {
          "text": "slave",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "bold"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": ", with the master's enable wired to CLK inverted and the slave's enable wired to CLK directly. Fifteen gates total, and every one is a NAND or a NOT you already know."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "code-master-slave",
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "CLK = 0:  master enable = 1 (transparent), slave enable = 0 (holding)\n            master follows D, slave holds its last value\n\nCLK = 1:  master enable = 0 (holding), slave enable = 1 (transparent)\n            master freezes whatever D was an instant before CLK rose,\n            slave immediately copies that frozen value out to Q"
    },
    {
      "id": "p-3",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Because the two enables are inverted copies of each other, they can never both be transparent at the same time. That's what turns \"two level-sensitive latches\" into one edge-sensitive flip-flop: at every instant exactly one of the two is open, and D can only reach Q by passing through both, master then slave, which only happens in the narrow window right as CLK transitions from low to high."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-sampling",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Try It: Sampling Once Per Tick"
    },
    {
      "id": "p-4",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Toggle D as many times as you like while CLK stays put, nothing happens to Q, because nothing you're touching is a clock edge. Now toggle CLK from 0 to 1 and back. Watch the master latch (left half) freeze the instant CLK rises, right as the slave latch (right half) opens and copies whatever the master was holding out to Q. That handoff, master closing exactly as slave opens, is the entire mechanism, laid out in gates instead of asserted as a black box."
        }
      ]
    },
